nodecode-bilinc is a rewrite of ReARCLabs's ReARCLabs/bilinc-hermes-plugin:

  https://github.com/ReARCLabs/bilinc-hermes-plugin
  commit 1ad81b4aaef8632ba5d6438384f3f063f46a0ebd
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 ReARC Labs (LICENSE)

The rewritten plugin is the repository root of that commit. Its four tools
(bilinc_recall, bilinc_remember, bilinc_revise, bilinc_forget) keep their names,
descriptions and parameters; its wire is carried over whole: POST
/api/cloud/memory/recall, /commit, /revise and /forget on https://bilinc.space,
Authorization: Bearer, an Idempotency-Key on every write, the same answer
shapes (results with key, value and entry_version; entryVersion on a write), and
an existing key refused rather than overwritten. So are its behaviour and its
defaults: automatic recall before a turn (recall_limit 6), capture of finished
turns as short-lived episodic records (off, 30 days), the [REDACTED] pass over
secret-shaped strings before anything is sent, the one-minute pause after a
failed recall, a rejected key leaving the tool to report what the API said, and
the block of words the model is given about it (bilinc_recall, bilinc_remember,
bilinc_revise, bilinc_forget; store only what will matter later, never secrets).

What was left out, and why: mirroring the host's built-in memory tool, because
Nodecode's own memory writes are not a hook a cell sees, so the model calls
bilinc_remember instead; the Python prefetch and writer threads and their queue,
because a Nodecode turn offers a :round point to inject into and the frame point
to fold turns from, so recall runs on the round's thread under a short ceiling
and capture on one worker; the hermes memory setup / status commands, whose
answers the cell's config section, /settings and /bilinc give; and the certifi
fallback, because the harness dials with the image's own trust store.

Three changes a reader of the wire should know: requests identify themselves as
bilinc-nodecode/0.1.0 with X-Bilinc-Client: nodecode-plugin, so Bilinc counts a
rewrite as a rewrite rather than as the original; keys the cell generates use
the prefixes nodecode_note:: and nodecode_turn:: (the original's hermes_note::
and hermes_session::); and a content digest is SBCL's own string hash rather
than SHA-256, which changes the generated key's spelling, not its stability.
LICENSE is the original repository's LICENSE, unchanged.
